| Custom kernel speedup papers (OqTVwjLlRI, OioOio3bmx) | 4.25 | Papers with custom kernel speedup claims but insufficient validation; scored low. R-Sparse has this weakness but also stronger accuracy results. |

R-Sparse makes a genuine and useful contribution — the input-sparsity + low-rank decomposition insight for LLMs is novel and well-motivated. However, two major weaknesses (unfair baseline comparison conflating architectural scope with algorithmic merit; FP32-only undocumented-kernel efficiency claims) prevent full confidence in the paper's headline claims. The paper is stronger than the medium-low scoring V:N:M/custom-kernel papers (4-5 range) because of its solid accuracy results and genuine conceptual contribution, but weaker than the topically similar ReLU Strikes Back (7.33) and Sparsity-Quantization Interplay (7.50) papers that had more complete experimental validation. Given the strength of the core insight but the significant gaps in validation, the paper sits in the border zone.

